  "abstract": "A double cone is obtained by adding two adjacent universal vertices to a finite simple graph, called its body. We prove that every biplanar double cone whose body has independence number at most three admits a nine-coloring. For a twenty-vertex body this gives a partition into six independent triples and an independent pair. Earlier results bound the body order by twenty-one, give the required coloring at order twenty-one and through order nineteen, and settle the twenty-vertex case through seventy-eight body edges. The last possible edge count is seventy-nine. We choose a vertex of degree seven and classify the graph on its twelve nonneighbors and the graph on its seven neighbors. Matchings among the nonneighbors, the structure of a clique of minimum-degree vertices, and cuts across two six-cliques reduce the possibilities to 9,632 primary pairs and 584 additional pairs. For each pair the 84 possible edges between the two vertex sets remain variable. We generate seven-color partitions and exclude every completion that destroys all of them. A second program checks the vertex labels and every partition, reconstructs the Boolean formulas, and verifies an unsatisfiability proof for each pair. The supplement provides the labeled inventory, proof records, and instructions for regenerating the partitions and proofs.",
